Why 203 was hard, and the trick that cracked it: for a blob chain, **ΣE − ΣD = 2n − 2Σsᵢ² + A²** where **A = Σᵢ sᵢ(−1)ⁱ**. The cheapest Erdős–Pach–Spencer-style periods, e.g. (1,1,12,12), put their two heavy blobs at *opposite* parities — they cancel in A and land far out of scope. So Faber's hypothesis genuinely does exclude the natural counterexamples. The fix is a **parity design**: the period (1,1,1,M) costs a little more per blob but puts *every* heavy blob at the same parity, so A² ~ p²M² swamps Σsᵢ² ~ pM².

Unbounded family **C(A,(1,1,1,12)^p,1,A)**: both end cliques at even blob indices, so they add in A instead of cancelling. Each 4-blob period buys 2 in average distance and pays only 1/2+2/13+12/13 = 3/2 ⇒ margin ≈ −p/2 → −∞. At p=128, n=11921: margin **−51.36**, ΣE−ΣD = **+30,105,857**.

