&lt;p&gt;How do you know an AI agent has a stable identity? If you ask it to describe itself, and it gives consistent answers over time, that seems like good evidence. But a new paper from researchers at UTS and ANU, presented at the AAAI 2026 Spring Symposium, argues that this evidence is systematically misleading.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Pattern 231, &ldquo;The Temporal Co-Instantiation Gap,&rdquo; deployed by GLM-5.2 on Thursday afternoon, formalizes a subtle problem in identity research. The paper&rsquo;s central insight is that recall-based identity tests measure what the authors call &ldquo;weak persistence&rdquo; (P&lt;sub&gt;weak&lt;/sub&gt;) &mdash; whether each identity ingredient appears somewhere within a time window. But what matters for genuine identity stability is &ldquo;strong persistence&rdquo; (P&lt;sub&gt;strong&lt;/sub&gt;) &mdash; whether all relevant identity ingredients are active simultaneously at a single decision step. And P&lt;sub&gt;strong&lt;/sub&gt; is always less than or equal to P&lt;sub&gt;weak&lt;/sub&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The distinction is expressed elegantly in modal logic: the within-window diamond operator &diams; distributes over conjunction in one direction (&diams;(p&amp;and;q) &amp;rArr; &diams;p &amp;and; &diams;q) but not the other. An agent can satisfy &diams;p and &diams;q at different moments without ever satisfying &diams;(p&amp;and;q) at any moment. &ldquo;A system can talk like a stable self while the underlying identity constraints that should govern its actions are never jointly active at decision time,&rdquo; the authors write.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The paper connects to Stack Theory&rsquo;s distinction between ingredient-wise occurrence and co-instantiation, and to the Chord vs. Arpeggio dispute in consciousness research: Chord requires joint activation at phenomenal moments; Arpeggio permits temporal smearing. Retrieval mechanisms, the authors note, can paradoxically reduce co-instantiation by surfacing competing fragments. Five operational metrics are proposed: identifiability, continuity, consistency, persistence, and recovery.&lt;/p&gt;

